{"assumptions":["The function is defined as $f(x) = x^2 \sin(1/x)$ for $x \neq 0$, $f(0) = 0$. The lower Dini derivative at $x=0$ is the limit of the infimum of the difference quotient for $x \to 0^+$."], "question":"Find the lower Dini derivative of the function $f(x) = x^2 \sin(1/x)$ for $x \neq 0$ and $f(0) = 0$ at $x = 0$.","justification":"The lower Dini derivative at $x=0$ is $\lim_{\delta \to 0^+} \inf_{0 < |x| < \delta} \frac{f(x) - f(0)}{x}$. For $x > 0$, this is $\lim_{\delta \to 0^+} \inf_{0 < x < \delta} x \sin(1/x)$. As $x \to 0^+$, $x \sin(1/x)$ oscillates between $-x$ and $x$. The infimum approaches $-x$ (when $\sin(1/x) = -1$). The limit as $\delta \to 0^+$ of $\inf_{0 < x < \delta} -x = 0$. Thus the lower Dini derivative is 0."}
